  [1mError handling.[0m [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is not guaranteed to work. Access to it can throw outright in sandboxed iframes, and
  writes can throw on quota or when storage is disabled. So the module wraps both the feature detection and every
  read/write in [38;5;153mtry[39m/[38;5;153mcatch[39m, and falls back to a module-level variable holding the id in memory. The fallback degrades
  honestly: the id survives navigation within the page's lifetime but not a reload, and [38;5;153mSession.get()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m
  instead of throwing. No consumer needs to know which mode it's in.

  Two smaller cases: [38;5;153mSession.set[39m ignores [38;5;153mnull[39m/[38;5;153mundefined[39m rather than storing the string [38;5;153m"undefined"[39m, and the submit
  handler only calls [38;5;153mSession.set[39m when [38;5;153mresult.success[39m is true — today's handler logs the result unconditionally, and I'm
  not changing that logging, just gating the store on success.

  [1mFiles touched — three, all small:[0m

  - [38;5;153msession.js[39m [3m(new, ~30 lines)[0m — the [38;5;153mSession[39m global, the three functions, the storage fallback.
  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns [38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m with a clearly-marked placeholder id; the submit handler calls
    [38;5;153mSession.set(result.userId)[39m on success. Signature unchanged, [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m untouched.
  - [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — one [38;5;153m<script src="session.js">[39m tag before the existing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m tag. Load order matters: [38;5;153mSession[39m must
    exist before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m runs.

  [1mVerification.[0m With no test runner, this is manual and I'll say so in the report rather than implying coverage I don't
  have. Open [38;5;153mindex.html[39m in a browser, submit the form, confirm the console shows the id and that [38;5;153mSession.get()[39m in the
  console returns it. Reload the page and confirm [38;5;153mSession.get()[39m still returns it — that's the "persists" requirement
  actually demonstrated. Then [38;5;153mSession.clear()[39m, confirm [38;5;153mget()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m. I can't easily exercise the storage-fallback
  branch by hand, so that path ships reasoned-about but unverified; that's the concrete cost of skipping the test
  runner.

  [1mRisk worth naming once:[0m the placeholder id. Until [38;5;153mlogin[39m makes a real call to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, every session stores the
  same fake value, so "track who logged in" won't actually distinguish users yet. The plumbing will be correct and the
  swap is one line inside [38;5;153mlogin[39m, but the feature isn't truly delivered until the real API call exists. If you'd rather I
  wire up the actual [38;5;153mfetch[39m to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m as part of this, say so — that's a larger change and I'd want to design the
  failure handling for it.

  If this looks right, I'll write it up as a spec at [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-user-session-store-design.md[39m
  (uncommitted), run the review gates over it, and hand it to you to read before any code gets written.

